1
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:17,268
[SPEAKER_00]: The other day I mentioned to you my translation for upādāna in terms of pañca, upādāna, khandhā, the five assumed aggregates.

2
00:00:18,249 --> 00:00:25,094
[SPEAKER_00]: And I want to use the word bridge for upādāna, the five bridged aggregates.

3
00:00:27,255 --> 00:00:35,662
[SPEAKER_00]: So they would convey the meaning of what upādāna is, in a way, that connection between, let's say,

4
00:00:37,142 --> 00:00:47,330
[SPEAKER_00]: The body and mind, the in-between, the thing that connects them, and that, that bridges an imagination or...

5
00:00:48,151 --> 00:00:48,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Assumption.

6
00:00:48,791 --> 00:00:49,331
[SPEAKER_00]: An assumption.

7
00:00:49,672 --> 00:00:49,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

8
00:00:51,353 --> 00:00:57,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I mean, you can, you could, but if you translate it like that, you would have to explain it a lot more.

9
00:00:58,779 --> 00:01:06,605
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, whichever way you were to translate upādāna needs to be explained, because obviously the putujana's views are already, well,

10
00:01:07,172 --> 00:01:10,014
[SPEAKER_01]: It's upādāna that makes them putujana.

11
00:01:10,635 --> 00:01:17,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Putujana is putujana because of his upādāna, because of his assumption, because of his mental bridges, so to speak.

12
00:01:19,521 --> 00:01:32,711
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to look at what the nature of upādāna is, and then find a word that could possibly convey that, regardless of the view that's reading it.

13
00:01:33,232 --> 00:01:34,713
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean everybody will understand it.

14
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:39,201
[SPEAKER_01]: But it means that if they were to understand it, then they'll see the point of that translation.

15
00:01:40,101 --> 00:01:42,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I don't think the bridge necessarily would do that.

16
00:01:42,082 --> 00:01:45,803
[SPEAKER_01]: But it certainly can enrich the description of it.

17
00:01:45,823 --> 00:01:46,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the five aggregates.

18
00:01:47,583 --> 00:01:49,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Aggregates, for example, that's another translation.

19
00:01:49,224 --> 00:01:50,164
[SPEAKER_01]: We spoke about it before.

20
00:01:51,344 --> 00:01:53,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Khandhā, literally, I think, means heap.

21
00:01:54,345 --> 00:01:55,186
[SPEAKER_01]: It's five heaps.

22
00:01:56,726 --> 00:01:59,667
[SPEAKER_01]: So, five heaps is five different things heaped together.

23
00:01:59,947 --> 00:02:01,027
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't become one thing.

24
00:02:01,367 --> 00:02:02,007
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't merge.

25
00:02:02,047 --> 00:02:02,888
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't come together.

26
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:09,855
[SPEAKER_01]: With the assumption in place, yeah, you sort of... you bridge.

27
00:02:10,215 --> 00:02:20,664
[SPEAKER_01]: You bridge them together into this kind of morphed thing, where you think it's, you know, my feeling, my perception, it's all coming out of the same place, my sense of self, my action.

28
00:02:20,864 --> 00:02:26,649
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you get this whole sense of, like, directionality of the experience, me towards the world.

29
00:02:28,611 --> 00:02:29,792
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you actually start seeing...

30
00:02:30,694 --> 00:02:39,242
[SPEAKER_01]: When the Buddha was doing the analysis, he wasn't doing the analysis for the sake of abstract satisfaction of some theoretical knowledge.

31
00:02:40,143 --> 00:02:46,308
[SPEAKER_01]: He was more like discerning that there are actually three layers of your experience, in terms of action, for example.

32
00:02:46,328 --> 00:02:52,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Or there are like three different layers of feeling, simultaneously, as a possibility, present.

33
00:02:53,395 --> 00:02:56,638
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like I feel this, then I feel that.

34
00:02:57,191 --> 00:03:01,094
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just this feeling is there, then this feeling is there, then this feeling is there.

35
00:03:02,135 --> 00:03:05,578
[SPEAKER_01]: And pleasant feeling is independent of an unpleasant feeling.

36
00:03:06,339 --> 00:03:09,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasant and unpleasant feeling are independent of neither pleasant nor unpleasant feeling.

37
00:03:10,402 --> 00:03:15,306
[SPEAKER_01]: In other way, whatever phenomenon arises, arises in itself, for itself, so to speak.

38
00:03:16,127 --> 00:03:23,033
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't use that in order to justify some, as I said, some theoretical outlook, how everything is just...

39
00:03:24,133 --> 00:03:25,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Out there, not yours.

40
00:03:25,373 --> 00:03:27,294
[SPEAKER_01]: You use that to see the things that are yours.

41
00:03:27,954 --> 00:03:36,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Your sense of ownership, your sense of this is mine, these five aggregates, or these feelings, or these actions, can only be appropriated because they have arisen on their own.

42
00:03:37,237 --> 00:03:44,399
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize that... you get to realize their independence from your sense of ownership by seeing that it's three of them.

43
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:47,200
[SPEAKER_01]: It's five of them.

44
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:47,960
[SPEAKER_01]: It's two of them.

45
00:03:48,541 --> 00:03:49,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's just one of them.

46
00:03:50,698 --> 00:03:55,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Because one of the main characteristics of the sense of self is, it's that kind of, eh... single.

47
00:03:56,582 --> 00:03:56,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Unity.

48
00:03:57,143 --> 00:03:57,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Unity.

49
00:03:57,643 --> 00:04:02,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, my, I, oh, I take this, and I am that single center.

50
00:04:03,147 --> 00:04:05,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I feel like this, I feel like that.

51
00:04:05,108 --> 00:04:16,716
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start putting emphasis on the present feeling, then you realize, well, I feel like this, I feel like this, or feel like that, because that's what, eh... that is which feeling has arisen.

52
00:04:17,417 --> 00:04:18,778
[SPEAKER_01]: It determines how the self feels.

53
00:04:19,153 --> 00:04:20,034
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the Buddha meant.

54
00:04:20,374 --> 00:04:21,355
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which one is it then?

55
00:04:22,816 --> 00:04:33,964
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if... if you say, my sense of self... my... my self feels pleasure, my self... which... my self feels only pleasant feeling, how can that self remain standing when there is unpleasant feeling?

56
00:04:34,025 --> 00:04:35,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, my self feels all the feelings.

57
00:04:35,706 --> 00:04:39,489
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, yeah, but it's those feelings that arise first, that then your self feels them.

58
00:04:40,289 --> 00:04:41,811
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you say they're yours?

59
00:04:41,871 --> 00:04:43,152
[SPEAKER_01]: How can they say they belong to you?

60
00:04:43,912 --> 00:04:47,355
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of the aggregates, the division of, like, five aggregates,

61
00:04:47,995 --> 00:04:54,523
[SPEAKER_01]: It's to see that there are five heaps at the foundation of any experience you might be going through.

62
00:04:54,583 --> 00:05:00,970
[SPEAKER_01]: By being five, well, five components remain like means five components of a unit.

63
00:05:01,331 --> 00:05:08,099
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't want to be coming from a point of view of unit, because that means you're coming from a point of view of sense of self, which is that unit.

64
00:05:09,055 --> 00:05:13,359
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when people do investigate the five aggregates as components of their sense of self.

65
00:05:13,739 --> 00:05:19,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you want to see that these five random heaps are the necessary basis for your sense of self.

66
00:05:20,564 --> 00:05:28,071
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how sense of self cannot remain holding on to that assumed, completely gratuitously assumed, self-centralized position.

67
00:05:28,631 --> 00:05:30,773
[SPEAKER_01]: As if I am the master of these five

68
00:05:31,303 --> 00:05:32,144
[SPEAKER_01]: Five components.

69
00:05:32,224 --> 00:05:33,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, my experience.

70
00:05:33,465 --> 00:05:34,105
[SPEAKER_00]: This is my experience.

71
00:05:34,145 --> 00:05:35,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, this is my experience.

72
00:05:35,346 --> 00:05:36,086
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at...

73
00:05:36,106 --> 00:05:37,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, my experience.

74
00:05:37,147 --> 00:05:37,908
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't deny that.

75
00:05:38,148 --> 00:05:39,248
[SPEAKER_01]: This experience is mine.

76
00:05:39,729 --> 00:05:43,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Within that, you discern five simultaneously present heaps.

77
00:05:44,752 --> 00:05:48,175
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, if these five heaps, they cannot be four or three or one.

78
00:05:49,095 --> 00:05:49,896
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always five.

79
00:05:50,376 --> 00:05:52,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Means none of them can take centralized position.

80
00:05:53,278 --> 00:05:54,739
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, they can in the assumption.

81
00:05:55,860 --> 00:06:00,463
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why assumption is a gratuitous sort of parasite upon the structure of the five.

82
00:06:02,020 --> 00:06:13,548
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you realize that even when you assume, you can only assume because five were given beforehand, which means they're independent of your assumption, which means they're independent of the results of your assumption, which is the sense of self.

83
00:06:14,769 --> 00:06:16,911
[SPEAKER_01]: So then you realize when the sense of self is not yours.

84
00:06:18,132 --> 00:06:25,177
[SPEAKER_01]: This my experience is my, cannot be my, because it's actually rooted in these five random things, that have a reason on their own simultaneously present.

85
00:06:27,490 --> 00:06:28,610
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to see there...

86
00:06:28,810 --> 00:06:33,792
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to see the basis of five within that sense of ownership.

87
00:06:33,812 --> 00:06:38,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Not after, as an afterthought, or left right from it.

88
00:06:38,473 --> 00:06:42,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when you have a sense of ownership, you don't, like, okay, now I'm going to go and find these five things.

89
00:06:42,194 --> 00:06:42,374
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

90
00:06:42,774 --> 00:06:47,696
[SPEAKER_01]: When I have the sense of ownership, within that, I'm going to discern these five things.

91
00:06:47,716 --> 00:06:49,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Or three things, depending on your... or two things.

92
00:06:50,497 --> 00:06:54,198
[SPEAKER_01]: The diet, as the Buddha would describe it.

93
00:06:55,719 --> 00:07:00,322
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to see that whatever has arisen, has arisen on its own.

94
00:07:01,283 --> 00:07:02,224
[SPEAKER_01]: You are not its creator.

95
00:07:02,264 --> 00:07:06,887
[SPEAKER_01]: By not being its creator, your sense of ownership, that's undeniable for Bhutujana.

96
00:07:07,587 --> 00:07:10,469
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to see the sense of ownership as second, not as first.

97
00:07:11,230 --> 00:07:18,415
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you start to undo the ownership, when you see it as redundant, when you see it as not fundamental.

98
00:07:18,675 --> 00:07:20,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Because one doesn't see the separateness

99
00:07:23,281 --> 00:07:26,844
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see the separateness of each aggregate on the right basis.

100
00:07:27,044 --> 00:07:33,269
[SPEAKER_01]: You see it in abstract theory, which left that sense of ownership excluded from that theory.

101
00:07:34,210 --> 00:07:34,790
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

102
00:07:34,970 --> 00:07:43,277
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be investigating the five aggregates, but not on the terms of your ownership, but within the necessary basis of that ownership.

103
00:07:45,058 --> 00:07:45,839
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's also what

104
00:07:46,867 --> 00:07:52,991
[SPEAKER_01]: What differentiates abstract thinking from concrete thinking and reflection and contemplation?

105
00:07:57,273 --> 00:08:01,015
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of, like, body, speech and mind, there's three types of actions.

106
00:08:01,075 --> 00:08:02,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

107
00:08:02,316 --> 00:08:08,420
[SPEAKER_01]: There are three simultaneously present domains, independent of each other, in any living experience as a whole.

108
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:13,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you're smart or dumb, educated or not,

109
00:08:14,573 --> 00:08:17,235
[SPEAKER_01]: There are always three different domains.

110
00:08:17,516 --> 00:08:21,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Like three different layers, it's what constitutes your sense of self that acts.

111
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:25,923
[SPEAKER_01]: A layer of physical, a layer of verbal, a layer of mental.

112
00:08:26,644 --> 00:08:33,030
[SPEAKER_01]: And they don't merge, they don't come into each other, they don't cross from one into the other.

113
00:08:33,050 --> 00:08:34,351
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nothing that goes from...

114
00:08:35,872 --> 00:08:40,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Mental domain kind of flows into them and becomes part of the verbal domain.

115
00:08:40,917 --> 00:08:41,938
[SPEAKER_01]: You have mental thought,

116
00:08:42,231 --> 00:08:45,693
[SPEAKER_01]: That then results in breaking of a certain speech in a verbal domain.

117
00:08:46,753 --> 00:08:49,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Or results in a breaking of a certain action in a physical domain.

118
00:08:51,255 --> 00:08:58,858
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in... like, when people try to... the closest to the correlation you can get is with this, this is.

119
00:08:59,479 --> 00:09:03,721
[SPEAKER_01]: With this type of thought, this is the leaning of the physical domain towards that action.

120
00:09:04,221 --> 00:09:09,763
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is nothing that bridges them together, except the assumption of the bridge together.

121
00:09:10,644 --> 00:09:11,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Except the upādāna.

122
00:09:13,017 --> 00:09:14,718
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the point here.

123
00:09:14,738 --> 00:09:21,620
[SPEAKER_01]: To realize that when people try to investigate things, they usually end up looking for correlation.

124
00:09:23,841 --> 00:09:28,763
[SPEAKER_01]: And often, try to use the Buddha's description of paticca upādā.

125
00:09:29,843 --> 00:09:33,205
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not incidental that then paticca upādā becomes cause and effect.

126
00:09:34,445 --> 00:09:39,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So that correlation of these two simultaneously present things becomes their causation, literally.

127
00:09:41,127 --> 00:09:43,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's when this happens, then this happens.

128
00:09:44,310 --> 00:09:49,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, with this happens, this can happen, or even this could happen.

129
00:09:49,714 --> 00:09:54,497
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is that there is no connection between these two simultaneous to present phenomena.

130
00:09:54,517 --> 00:10:01,042
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, if you start discerning this, you start discerning the principle of simultaneity, which is what Bhatishthana Pada is.

131
00:10:02,323 --> 00:10:04,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, with this, this is.

132
00:10:05,326 --> 00:10:06,206
[SPEAKER_01]: That is undeniable.

133
00:10:06,687 --> 00:10:09,769
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the closest that that correlation can come.

134
00:10:10,457 --> 00:10:15,381
[SPEAKER_01]: But for an ignorant mind, mind that comes from the point of view of assumption, that's invisible.

135
00:10:15,541 --> 00:10:18,203
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for them, this becomes this.

136
00:10:19,064 --> 00:10:31,853
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I have the intention to sit down, and then there's the intention in the mind, and then electrons or whatever start firing up...

137
00:10:31,873 --> 00:10:32,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

138
00:10:32,374 --> 00:10:35,176
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that, none of that can be object of perception, which means

139
00:10:36,298 --> 00:10:37,399
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the basis then?

140
00:10:37,419 --> 00:10:37,960
[SPEAKER_01]: You ask yourself.

141
00:10:37,980 --> 00:10:45,748
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the basis for me having all these views that, oh, I have this intention and then the the the electrons were firing and that's why my arm is moving and doing this.

142
00:10:45,828 --> 00:10:46,809
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you even know that?

143
00:10:47,009 --> 00:10:54,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Except in this abstract theory that you use to bridge that gap between this intention, this action.

144
00:10:54,837 --> 00:10:55,538
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all you can say.

145
00:10:55,558 --> 00:10:57,620
[SPEAKER_00]: I have...

146
00:10:58,100 --> 00:10:59,502
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm doing something because there is.

147
00:11:00,252 --> 00:11:02,314
[SPEAKER_01]: There is that intention present in my mind.

148
00:11:02,794 --> 00:11:03,434
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean.

149
00:11:03,935 --> 00:11:08,879
[SPEAKER_01]: And it, like, it might seem almost like a minor thing, but it's actually, it's actually the most crucial one.

150
00:11:08,899 --> 00:11:11,701
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha described bodhisattva as the most fundamental principle.

151
00:11:12,361 --> 00:11:27,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you discern, if you are able to discern the principle of two simultaneous things present together, without assuming any relationship between the two, means there won't be any conceiving between the two.

152
00:11:28,100 --> 00:11:32,742
[SPEAKER_01]: He conceives in, apart, from, both, neither, mine, whatever.

153
00:11:33,122 --> 00:11:40,325
[SPEAKER_01]: He assumes relationship with it, which means, ah, you, you get to see them related, which is already a form of connection.

154
00:11:41,285 --> 00:11:50,889
[SPEAKER_01]: The slightest form of relationship, even if it's a relationship of denial, I deny this, I deny the existence of this, it's already a form of relationship, but that, as such, means it's already a connection.

155
00:11:53,770 --> 00:11:57,292
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of the action, you have, say, you have a thought to eat something.

156
00:11:59,030 --> 00:12:00,111
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you're actually eating it.

157
00:12:02,053 --> 00:12:10,360
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mind is affected by ignorance, if the mind has not developed sufficient basis of necessary amount of mindfulness, you will think it is the same thing.

158
00:12:11,641 --> 00:12:17,046
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already a form of assumed identity between the thought and the physical action.

159
00:12:18,627 --> 00:12:24,853
[SPEAKER_01]: And whenever there is any shape of assumed identity, means your sense of self is there to that extent.

160
00:12:27,613 --> 00:12:29,274
[SPEAKER_01]: To that extent, you're appropriating it.

161
00:12:29,354 --> 00:12:31,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Just through the extent of lack of clarity.

162
00:12:31,716 --> 00:12:35,459
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't necessarily have to think explicitly.

163
00:12:35,519 --> 00:12:36,300
[SPEAKER_01]: This is mine.

164
00:12:36,700 --> 00:12:37,561
[SPEAKER_01]: This is my action.

165
00:12:37,601 --> 00:12:38,441
[SPEAKER_01]: This is my food.

166
00:12:38,862 --> 00:12:39,502
[SPEAKER_01]: I am eating.

167
00:12:39,602 --> 00:12:40,123
[SPEAKER_01]: I am eating.

168
00:12:40,203 --> 00:12:40,343
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

169
00:12:40,943 --> 00:12:46,428
[SPEAKER_01]: It's enough to think that your thought of eating food and physically eating food are the same thing.

170
00:12:47,508 --> 00:12:51,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Or then, you start thinking, oh no, these are completely different things, as in two separate identities.

171
00:12:52,874 --> 00:12:56,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you either overshoot or undershoot the mark.

172
00:12:56,676 --> 00:13:02,799
[SPEAKER_01]: You are unable to see them, to the extent they are risen, that they are risen.

173
00:13:02,859 --> 00:13:09,442
[SPEAKER_01]: You're unable to see them, that with the presently enduring thought of eating food, there is the physical eating of food.

174
00:13:11,143 --> 00:13:12,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Two separate domains.

175
00:13:13,324 --> 00:13:22,529
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when these things arise, it will be... it will require... develop mindfulness to not give in, rise to that natural,

176
00:13:23,689 --> 00:13:25,950
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no confusion and conflating of the two domains.

177
00:13:26,410 --> 00:13:28,391
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, thought of food means this food.

178
00:13:29,171 --> 00:13:35,194
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the nature of... that's how people are connected, so to speak, with their sensuality, with sensual cravings.

179
00:13:35,874 --> 00:13:38,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Because sensuality can never step outside of the physical domain.

180
00:13:38,796 --> 00:13:41,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it's fully governed by the mental assumption of sensuality.

181
00:13:42,097 --> 00:13:43,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not in physical.

182
00:13:43,578 --> 00:13:46,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality, it's not contained in physical objects of your senses.

183
00:13:47,520 --> 00:13:51,782
[SPEAKER_01]: It's contained in you assuming the connection between the two,

184
00:13:52,929 --> 00:13:59,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Thought of something pleasurable, engagement with my... of my body with that pleasurable, is that pursuit of that thought.

185
00:14:00,933 --> 00:14:07,296
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's that directional assumption that takes me from I want this, to I want to talk about it, to I want to have it physically.

186
00:14:08,517 --> 00:14:19,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see that wanting it on your mind... in your mind, verbally talking about it on level of speech, physically pursuing it, are three separate domains that can never come together.

187
00:14:19,502 --> 00:14:21,023
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you're going for that pleasure,

188
00:14:21,975 --> 00:14:23,997
[SPEAKER_01]: When all you see... Well, that's the thing.

189
00:14:24,057 --> 00:14:28,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality assumes that end result, which end result is merging of it all.

190
00:14:28,621 --> 00:14:31,805
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the whole notion of trying to satisfy your desire.

191
00:14:33,566 --> 00:14:38,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, idea of satisfaction is a hybrid of these three domains.

192
00:14:38,771 --> 00:14:39,212
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a view.

193
00:14:39,512 --> 00:14:40,133
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's what I mean.

194
00:14:40,453 --> 00:14:41,114
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the trash.

195
00:14:41,134 --> 00:14:42,315
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where putujana starts with.

196
00:14:42,415 --> 00:14:43,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Or somebody who's unfree from sensuality.

197
00:14:43,756 --> 00:14:44,717
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where they start with.

198
00:14:45,448 --> 00:14:47,350
[SPEAKER_01]: That hybrid is the first point.

199
00:14:47,370 --> 00:14:48,931
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about finding the way out of it.

200
00:14:48,951 --> 00:14:50,012
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about undoing it.

201
00:14:50,713 --> 00:15:10,931
[SPEAKER_01]: And you undo where you see that your hybrid of the view of satisfying your desires, it's actually simultaneously formed, determined by the three independent separate domains that are always simultaneously present.

202
00:15:12,297 --> 00:15:13,679
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why they're so hard to discern.

203
00:15:13,779 --> 00:15:17,203
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like one, then the two comes, then you get to see one fully... No.

204
00:15:17,464 --> 00:15:19,827
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always three, and they're always simultaneously present.

205
00:15:20,988 --> 00:15:22,230
[SPEAKER_01]: You can attend them differently.

206
00:15:22,630 --> 00:15:24,092
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't see them all at once.

207
00:15:25,334 --> 00:15:25,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

208
00:15:25,594 --> 00:15:27,857
[SPEAKER_01]: Domain of body, domain of speech, domain of the mind.

209
00:15:29,239 --> 00:15:40,366
[SPEAKER_01]: But the desire is the assumption, the end goal of that whole sort of directional connection of my thought, my speech, my action, my satisfaction.

210
00:15:40,906 --> 00:15:51,753
[SPEAKER_01]: But even people who are not free from sensuality, people who are not free from wrong view, even they know that there is a problem with that hybrid, which is sensuality, what sensuality is.

211
00:15:52,233 --> 00:15:58,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they, like, even the person who is the most bent on sensual pursuits,

212
00:15:59,357 --> 00:16:02,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Still knows deep down inside that you can't really satisfy your desires.

213
00:16:02,900 --> 00:16:04,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your desire is on...

214
00:16:04,281 --> 00:16:05,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Your desire is on the mental level.

215
00:16:05,683 --> 00:16:06,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, somewhere else.

216
00:16:06,443 --> 00:16:11,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, the only means of comprehending any form of satisfaction would be on a physical level.

217
00:16:12,128 --> 00:16:19,795
[SPEAKER_01]: If you give... if you... if you accept desire at a face value on the mental level, then you leave yourself no other option than to try to satisfy it on a physical level.

218
00:16:20,836 --> 00:16:22,157
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it's not the same thing.

219
00:16:22,557 --> 00:16:23,418
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a completely different thing.

220
00:16:25,051 --> 00:16:28,073
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why sensuality requires ignorance.

221
00:16:28,573 --> 00:16:35,097
[SPEAKER_01]: It requires a blind eye applied to this very nature that, actually, these are two separate things.

222
00:16:36,658 --> 00:16:50,467
[SPEAKER_01]: And physically scratching it, and even getting the satisfaction from scratching, requires you to not look closely and see that, yeah, that's one thing, but my mental desire projection towards the endgame of sensuality is a completely different thing, and it's still there.

223
00:16:51,609 --> 00:16:59,433
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you need that belief of sensuality, whereby, when physically scratching becomes unpleasant, that's when you feel satisfied.

224
00:16:59,974 --> 00:17:03,156
[SPEAKER_01]: But the thought of you wanting that satisfaction hasn't changed.

225
00:17:03,876 --> 00:17:05,797
[SPEAKER_01]: You only forgot about it at a time.

226
00:17:07,998 --> 00:17:16,603
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your whole point of sensual pursuit, of sensual projection, is to get rid of the pressure of the desire on the mental level, why not address it right there?

227
00:17:16,803 --> 00:17:19,425
[SPEAKER_01]: And stop bothering with physical pursuit, which is clearly

228
00:17:20,212 --> 00:17:23,473
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a, like a, like a sidestepping, taking you away elsewhere.

229
00:17:23,513 --> 00:17:27,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Different domain can have no influence on the mental domain, in that sense.

230
00:17:27,815 --> 00:17:32,676
[SPEAKER_00]: This domain is craving, so you go to another domain to try and sort it out.

231
00:17:32,696 --> 00:17:33,957
[SPEAKER_01]: To try and sort out the craving.

232
00:17:34,097 --> 00:17:40,899
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only reason you can stay engaged in that other domain, is if the craving remains present on the mental domain.

233
00:17:41,099 --> 00:17:41,739
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

234
00:17:41,759 --> 00:17:44,300
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it even contradicts that very attempt.

235
00:17:45,481 --> 00:17:49,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, in order to go to another domain, you need the presence of craving in the mental domain.

236
00:17:50,462 --> 00:17:53,584
[SPEAKER_01]: But you go there to try to get rid of the craving in the mental domain.

237
00:17:56,507 --> 00:17:59,869
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why the physical restraint is not optional, it has to come first, always.

238
00:18:00,489 --> 00:18:03,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's a complete misapplication.

239
00:18:04,462 --> 00:18:09,745
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just have to stop doing it in order to see where the problem really is, which is on the mental domain.

240
00:18:09,825 --> 00:18:12,687
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you see the desire, you see that, you see those two domains.

241
00:18:12,767 --> 00:18:12,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

242
00:18:13,107 --> 00:18:15,809
[SPEAKER_01]: You see the desire and then you see the pressure.

243
00:18:16,570 --> 00:18:24,775
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that pressure towards physical action or verbal action, output, you realize, oh, well, even that pressure, it's still on the mental level.

244
00:18:25,035 --> 00:18:31,739
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way that physical or verbal will be manifested is if I act out of it.

245
00:18:32,890 --> 00:18:34,991
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only shape and form that this can manifest.

246
00:18:35,531 --> 00:18:39,593
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you can have all the desire you want in your mind, pressuring you to do something physically.

247
00:18:40,473 --> 00:18:48,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you're actively deciding to do something physically, there is nothing manifested in a physical domain on account of that desire, because you haven't done anything.

248
00:18:51,038 --> 00:18:55,961
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nothing manifested in a verbal domain on account of a mental desire to say something, because you haven't said anything.

249
00:18:57,861 --> 00:19:01,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, oh, that's all I have to do in order to protect these two domains.

250
00:19:02,154 --> 00:19:10,176
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you do that, then that assumed... well, that pressure that comes out of that assumption... because again, as I said, it's about undoing that hybrid assumption.

251
00:19:11,057 --> 00:19:18,519
[SPEAKER_01]: And until that's undone, there's going to be a degree of pressure towards physical or towards verbal domain, coming from your mental craving.

252
00:19:19,139 --> 00:19:21,700
[SPEAKER_01]: But the more you undo it, the less pressure there is.

253
00:19:22,300 --> 00:19:25,461
[SPEAKER_01]: The less pressure there is, the clearer the nature becomes.

254
00:19:26,035 --> 00:19:32,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there is a thought of this that used to be governed by the promise of pleasure in the physical domain.

255
00:19:32,798 --> 00:19:34,759
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that promise of pleasure was fully mental.

256
00:19:34,779 --> 00:19:37,000
[SPEAKER_01]: It can never be really the physical pleasure.

257
00:19:37,340 --> 00:19:40,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the only way to have physical pleasure is to physically have pleasure.

258
00:19:41,022 --> 00:19:46,184
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you have a desire, a mental desire towards physical pleasure, that's a physical absence.

259
00:19:47,605 --> 00:19:51,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mental desire for physical pleasure means there is no actual physical pleasure.

260
00:19:52,692 --> 00:19:56,475
[SPEAKER_01]: But we act as if that mental desire, physical pleasure, is on the physical level.

261
00:19:56,896 --> 00:19:59,999
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all I need to do is extend my arm and take and touch what I want to have.

262
00:20:01,520 --> 00:20:15,453
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it reminds me of this, like, if I get frustrated, if someone does something to me, and there, in my mind, I'm angry, I'm frustrated, and so I don't shout, I restrain myself.

263
00:20:16,594 --> 00:20:18,435
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, there's this view,

264
00:20:19,428 --> 00:20:21,409
[SPEAKER_00]: I must get out the anger somehow.

265
00:20:21,449 --> 00:20:26,592
[SPEAKER_00]: I must get out the frustration, because keeping it in my mind is going to give me cancer.

266
00:20:26,652 --> 00:20:27,693
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to destroy me.

267
00:20:28,333 --> 00:20:32,735
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I must get it out somehow, by saying, by ranting, by telling someone else.

268
00:20:33,015 --> 00:20:33,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what I mean.

269
00:20:33,836 --> 00:20:36,097
[SPEAKER_00]: But your your anger doesn't come out of your mind.

270
00:20:36,197 --> 00:20:47,023
[SPEAKER_01]: I must get out because the pressure... Well, you mistake the pressure that was revealed by your restraint, you mistake it to be caused by your restraint.

271
00:20:48,450 --> 00:20:58,512
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a person who restrains their anger, physically and verbally, has revealed the pressure of the mental frustration that wanted to express itself through anger.

272
00:20:59,412 --> 00:20:59,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Reveals it.

273
00:20:59,913 --> 00:21:00,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

274
00:21:00,353 --> 00:21:00,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Reveals it.

275
00:21:00,953 --> 00:21:07,814
[SPEAKER_01]: But, people would think that, oh, this pressure wasn't there, as in, I wasn't aware of it before, because before, whenever I thought something, I would say something.

276
00:21:07,854 --> 00:21:10,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever I thought something, I would do something.

277
00:21:11,315 --> 00:21:15,496
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, I stopped doing it, stopped saying it, and now I have all this pressure that I'm not giving in to.

278
00:21:16,260 --> 00:21:19,505
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they think that that pressure has been caused by not giving in to it.

279
00:21:19,686 --> 00:21:21,649
[SPEAKER_01]: But it hasn't.

280
00:21:21,769 --> 00:21:28,360
[SPEAKER_01]: If that pressure were not there, regardless of your restraint or not, you wouldn't have never acted out of anger.

281
00:21:29,810 --> 00:21:33,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Mental anger would never make you do something physically, or say something verbally.

282
00:21:34,635 --> 00:21:36,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, that's how you used to act.

283
00:21:36,558 --> 00:21:46,268
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, when you start restraining that action, you realize that, oh, the pressure that I became aware of it now, has been revealed by my restraint, as something that was always there.

284
00:21:46,989 --> 00:21:50,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Not caused by my restraint, as something that wasn't there before.

285
00:21:51,802 --> 00:21:54,503
[SPEAKER_01]: And when that view changes, that's when you start valuing the restraint.

286
00:21:55,164 --> 00:22:00,046
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as you don't change that view, you will blame restraint for the pressure that got revealed.

287
00:22:00,266 --> 00:22:04,548
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll think, pressure got created, I better release it, because I will implode.

288
00:22:07,190 --> 00:22:07,730
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's what I mean.

289
00:22:07,750 --> 00:22:12,932
[SPEAKER_01]: You only need to... you don't need to look further than just... just see how were you behaving when you don't need to restrain yourself.

290
00:22:13,433 --> 00:22:14,853
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize there's still that pressure there.

291
00:22:15,233 --> 00:22:18,315
[SPEAKER_01]: You just don't feel it, because you get to turn a blind eye to it, because you get to act.

292
00:22:19,389 --> 00:22:21,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Physically and verbally, because there is no restraint.

293
00:22:21,710 --> 00:22:22,490
[SPEAKER_00]: So you see the pressure...

294
00:22:22,510 --> 00:22:23,090
[SPEAKER_01]: So you don't...

295
00:22:23,110 --> 00:22:24,991
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a pressure build-up, quickly release it.

296
00:22:25,011 --> 00:22:25,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

297
00:22:25,931 --> 00:22:28,952
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a pressure build-up, I won't release it, pressure builds up.

298
00:22:29,772 --> 00:22:32,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you restrained, you saw the pressure that's been building up.

299
00:22:33,633 --> 00:22:35,974
[SPEAKER_01]: But whether you restrain or not, pressure will be building up.

300
00:22:36,914 --> 00:22:41,416
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you take pressure to be the problem, you realize restraint is not an option.

301
00:22:41,656 --> 00:22:42,316
[SPEAKER_01]: It must be done.

302
00:22:43,016 --> 00:22:45,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Because how can I deal with the pressure if I keep releasing it right away?

303
00:22:46,237 --> 00:22:46,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Every second.

304
00:22:46,937 --> 00:22:49,158
[SPEAKER_01]: As soon as it starts building up, I quickly, quickly open everything.

305
00:22:50,490 --> 00:22:55,874
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you're trying to deal with the arising of the pressure.

306
00:22:57,975 --> 00:23:05,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to deal with the arising of the pressure, is to let the pressure arise, and not leak it out.

307
00:23:07,741 --> 00:23:08,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Let it be.

308
00:23:08,942 --> 00:23:09,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

309
00:23:09,602 --> 00:23:11,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Let it be, then you get to see where pressure has come from.

310
00:23:12,044 --> 00:23:14,045
[SPEAKER_01]: But, oh, is there... oh, pressure's gone.

311
00:23:14,185 --> 00:23:16,687
[SPEAKER_01]: I have to wait for new pressure to come, to see where it's coming from.

312
00:23:18,617 --> 00:23:23,639
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you keep letting the pressure out, how can you see where it's being built up from?

313
00:23:23,659 --> 00:23:28,760
[SPEAKER_00]: First thing, the pressure builds up, builds up, builds up... And what?

314
00:23:29,220 --> 00:23:29,500
[SPEAKER_00]: And then?

315
00:23:29,640 --> 00:23:29,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

316
00:23:30,641 --> 00:23:36,583
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot... No amount of pressure build up can make you act in a different domain.

317
00:23:37,643 --> 00:23:39,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Mental pressure is mental pressure.

318
00:23:39,503 --> 00:23:45,085
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why always the responsibility for your action never changes.

319
00:23:45,165 --> 00:23:47,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if a person is sort of losing their mind, so to speak,

320
00:23:49,653 --> 00:23:52,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, if they choose to act physically or verbally, that's on them.

321
00:23:54,416 --> 00:24:01,680
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no amount of mental pressure build-up can make your hands, legs, head do things.

322
00:24:02,620 --> 00:24:05,121
[SPEAKER_01]: You choose that pressure to be the reason for you acting.

323
00:24:05,902 --> 00:24:11,185
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, inherently, kama, as a form of action, is based on ignorance.

324
00:24:11,845 --> 00:24:17,168
[SPEAKER_01]: It's based on that assumption, based on that bridge between the domains that are unbridgeable.

325
00:24:18,658 --> 00:24:23,741
[SPEAKER_01]: When you fully discern that these domains can never connect, you realize, oh, there is no pressure now.

326
00:24:24,561 --> 00:24:36,768
[SPEAKER_01]: No amount of mental... what used to cause pressure mentally, such as prospect of death, or prospect of some... well, some significant experience.

327
00:24:38,088 --> 00:24:44,392
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize even that pressure that were being built up in your mind was because there were traces of assumption of connection.

328
00:24:45,746 --> 00:24:51,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure build-up in the mind means there is a possibility of acting out of it on the physical or verbal domain.

329
00:24:51,809 --> 00:25:02,635
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove the possibility of acting out of it, as in, you remove the assumption of of the slightest connection between these domains, there is no pressure to arise.

330
00:25:03,716 --> 00:25:06,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if the highway robbers are chopping you limb by limb.

331
00:25:06,518 --> 00:25:12,701
[SPEAKER_00]: So, someone call... calls me name... yeah, calls me something, says something, swears at me or whatever, says I'm an idiot.

332
00:25:14,940 --> 00:25:19,422
[SPEAKER_00]: On that level, that domain, but then I get angry in my mind.

333
00:25:19,722 --> 00:25:19,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

334
00:25:20,622 --> 00:25:23,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of an external speech.

335
00:25:24,184 --> 00:25:24,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

336
00:25:26,585 --> 00:25:28,806
[SPEAKER_01]: What's going on?

337
00:25:29,586 --> 00:25:30,847
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, it's, it's, it's...

338
00:25:33,128 --> 00:25:38,530
[SPEAKER_01]: The anger, the hazarism, remain fully enclosed on a mental level.

339
00:25:39,390 --> 00:25:43,932
[SPEAKER_01]: But you didn't know how to deal with it, so then you jumped on a verbal level, or a physical level.

340
00:25:47,845 --> 00:25:55,191
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you know how to not be affected by that prospect of arising anger on the mental level, why would you be looking to express it anywhere else?

341
00:25:55,311 --> 00:25:56,452
[SPEAKER_01]: There would be nothing to express.

342
00:25:58,814 --> 00:26:07,640
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as you give in to a form of expressing anger through physical or verbal domains, means to that extent you're maintaining belief that anger is expressible.

343
00:26:08,441 --> 00:26:10,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Those domains, which means, how can you then uproot it?

344
00:26:11,263 --> 00:26:11,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Where anger is?

345
00:26:13,071 --> 00:26:14,632
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you might... You keep releasing the pressure.

346
00:26:14,652 --> 00:26:20,195
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep believing that releasing the pressure in completely unconnected domains is the way of dealing with pressure.

347
00:26:20,556 --> 00:26:23,737
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like revenge, like... or killing someone.

348
00:26:23,818 --> 00:26:24,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

349
00:26:24,638 --> 00:26:29,061
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the ultimate, sort of, extension of this perverted principle.

350
00:26:32,603 --> 00:26:41,048
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, you realize that if there is a pressure on the level of the mind, yeah, there is an assumption on the level of the mind that results in this pressure.

351
00:26:41,967 --> 00:26:51,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is uncomfortable, but you realize the only way to really deal with it is to treat it on the level of the mind, to overcome it.

352
00:26:51,912 --> 00:27:03,697
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what Buddha meant when he said, even if a person lives physically withdrawn from sensuality, unless his mind has been withdrawn from assuming the same, he's still wet.

353
00:27:06,159 --> 00:27:10,901
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you take the two sticks out of the water, but you still won't be able to light the fire with them, because they're still wet.

354
00:27:12,457 --> 00:27:19,241
[SPEAKER_01]: So, having been withdrawn physically, then now you need to dry out your mind from those same assumptions that kept making you act physically.

355
00:27:20,501 --> 00:27:29,686
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can use any form of pressure, any form of that mental discomfort, you can use it as a symptom of some humidity left, so to speak.

356
00:27:33,088 --> 00:27:35,590
[SPEAKER_01]: And when there is no pressure, means the sticks are completely dry.

357
00:27:35,790 --> 00:27:37,370
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you... it will be self-evident.

358
00:27:37,671 --> 00:27:41,753
[SPEAKER_00]: So, okay, so... where there once was

359
00:27:43,825 --> 00:27:44,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Pressure.

360
00:27:46,186 --> 00:27:47,887
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's your measurement there.

361
00:27:48,427 --> 00:27:48,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

362
00:27:48,727 --> 00:27:51,929
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're no longer... there's no longer that pressure to act.

363
00:27:52,829 --> 00:27:53,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

364
00:27:53,409 --> 00:27:54,230
[SPEAKER_00]: That is a sign.

365
00:27:56,190 --> 00:28:01,273
[SPEAKER_01]: That you are not misconceiving the nature of the three domains.

366
00:28:02,173 --> 00:28:05,655
[SPEAKER_01]: So... That is a sign that you are not as deluded as you were before.

367
00:28:08,276 --> 00:28:12,118
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you... so, you are... there is anger.

368
00:28:12,198 --> 00:28:13,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, see, even that, like,

369
00:28:14,347 --> 00:28:17,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger requires a degree of that pressure on the mental level.

370
00:28:17,368 --> 00:28:18,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, it's discomfort.

371
00:28:18,809 --> 00:28:25,651
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, that's why, like, the right view doesn't automatically jump into freedom from sensuality and anger.

372
00:28:27,152 --> 00:28:30,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that's why there is the step of sakadāgāmī in between.

373
00:28:30,453 --> 00:28:31,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Weakening of these things.

374
00:28:33,794 --> 00:28:35,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Weakening of these assumptions.

375
00:28:36,635 --> 00:28:37,936
[SPEAKER_00]: There's not much of a pressure.

376
00:28:38,456 --> 00:28:38,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

377
00:28:39,096 --> 00:28:40,536
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for somebody with the right view,

378
00:28:43,430 --> 00:28:53,573
[SPEAKER_01]: He's virtually unable, will be virtually unable to act to the same extent, out of anger or lust, as somebody who doesn't have the right view.

379
00:28:55,814 --> 00:29:00,336
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for sakadāgāmī, he will be even less able to act out of these things, but still to some extent.

380
00:29:00,756 --> 00:29:03,957
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, only anāgāmī cannot really act or say anything sensual.

381
00:29:04,697 --> 00:29:05,978
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how he uprooted it.

382
00:29:06,738 --> 00:29:07,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality.

383
00:29:07,678 --> 00:29:13,300
[SPEAKER_01]: But, on the mental level, there are still traces of certain lacks of clarity,

384
00:29:13,724 --> 00:29:20,053
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to certain much more refined perversions of the natural principles.

385
00:29:21,796 --> 00:29:25,562
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the Arahantship sort of gets developed, if you approve that.

386
00:29:28,078 --> 00:29:51,839
[SPEAKER_01]: So, having an angry thought for Sotāpanna, he might be emotionally even affected, but not to the extent of going and just killing, anarahant, harming people in that in that sense of like a full-on blind rage, which requires the sufficient amount of that belief in connecting these domains of mental and physical output.

387
00:29:53,637 --> 00:29:54,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Sakadāgāmī even less.

388
00:29:55,038 --> 00:30:09,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, maybe on some extremely, extremely careless days, too much, sort of, going on mindfulness lapsed for a long time, he might do things out of sensuality or anger, but obviously that's, like, miniscule in comparison to... And then anāgāmī becomes unable.

389
00:30:09,606 --> 00:30:17,730
[SPEAKER_01]: No amount of pressure, no amount of adverse circumstances, no amount of loss of mindfulness can make these domains connect through assumption again.

390
00:30:18,230 --> 00:30:23,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he dwelt on it long enough, that it became so clear that that's undoable now.

391
00:30:23,570 --> 00:30:25,772
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't undo the clarity.

392
00:30:27,033 --> 00:30:31,336
[SPEAKER_01]: Because those domains were truly unconnectable to begin with.

393
00:30:31,376 --> 00:30:36,040
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said, it's because the mind cannot really be polluted by these things.

394
00:30:36,340 --> 00:30:39,042
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why liberation from pollution of these things is possible.

395
00:30:39,062 --> 00:30:52,293
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when there's an unpleasant feeling, whatever is happening, say, someone is

396
00:30:53,411 --> 00:30:55,371
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, someone just stole something from you.

397
00:30:55,912 --> 00:30:57,392
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize this was stolen.

398
00:30:57,732 --> 00:30:58,972
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an unpleasant feeling.

399
00:30:59,753 --> 00:31:02,713
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't result in anger or fear.

400
00:31:03,113 --> 00:31:03,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

401
00:31:04,474 --> 00:31:05,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's just a disagreeable feeling.

402
00:31:06,174 --> 00:31:06,514
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

403
00:31:06,754 --> 00:31:08,395
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's, on a physical level, someone stole something.

404
00:31:08,415 --> 00:31:13,156
[SPEAKER_01]: All what we spoke about now, about the aggregates of the three types of action, you can say the same for feeling and for perception.

405
00:31:13,456 --> 00:31:14,256
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I was writing there.

406
00:31:14,296 --> 00:31:15,996
[SPEAKER_00]: I say feeling feels, perception perceives.

407
00:31:16,016 --> 00:31:16,777
[SPEAKER_00]: I perceive this.

408
00:31:16,877 --> 00:31:18,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Intention intends, in a way.

409
00:31:19,657 --> 00:31:22,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so your recent feeling requires an assumption

410
00:31:23,757 --> 00:31:29,779
[SPEAKER_01]: For you to be acting towards or against perceptions, because feeling is felt, perception is perceived.

411
00:31:30,880 --> 00:31:34,681
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that you perceive what you feel.

412
00:31:34,721 --> 00:31:39,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Like you... what you're perceiving is what you're feeling, so then you're dealing with what you're perceiving, that's how you deal with what you feel.

413
00:31:39,743 --> 00:31:39,883
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

414
00:31:40,083 --> 00:31:41,764
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't release your feelings into your perception.

415
00:31:41,784 --> 00:31:45,706
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't release the feeling out of a domain of feeling into the domain of perception.

416
00:31:45,826 --> 00:31:47,927
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said when he asked him, what is feeling?

417
00:31:47,947 --> 00:31:50,067
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, feeling is that which feels.

418
00:31:50,087 --> 00:31:50,608
[SPEAKER_01]: What's perception?

419
00:31:50,628 --> 00:31:51,508
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that which perceives.

420
00:31:53,423 --> 00:31:54,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception perceives.

421
00:31:54,504 --> 00:31:55,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling feels.

422
00:31:55,144 --> 00:31:55,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Matter matters.

423
00:31:57,426 --> 00:31:58,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Consciousness cognizes.

424
00:31:58,707 --> 00:32:00,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentions intend.

425
00:32:00,189 --> 00:32:01,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you want to translate.

426
00:32:02,651 --> 00:32:03,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Five heaps.

427
00:32:04,812 --> 00:32:05,593
[SPEAKER_01]: It's five heaps.

428
00:32:05,693 --> 00:32:07,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like five things that merge into one thing.

429
00:32:09,056 --> 00:32:14,380
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no amount of the expanse of the heap, it's still gonna be five heaps.

430
00:32:15,221 --> 00:32:20,165
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when there is like five, say, five layers of different things, and then you have

431
00:32:21,748 --> 00:32:25,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Another five layers of the same kind of things, just double it up.

432
00:32:26,349 --> 00:32:34,452
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you say you have a... you have a towel, you have a stone, you have a bucket, you have a piece of wood and a piece of metal.

433
00:32:35,252 --> 00:32:42,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you have another towel, another bucket, another wood, another metal... Then, you have six towels and fifteen buckets and... and heap it, and heap it, and heap it.

434
00:32:42,174 --> 00:32:45,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Still, if you go in, you'll see towel, towel, towel, towel, towel, towel.

435
00:32:45,975 --> 00:32:51,317
[SPEAKER_01]: No amount of towels, it's still gonna be, oh, these are all heap of towels.

436
00:32:51,924 --> 00:32:55,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, domain of towels within the heap of these different things.

437
00:32:56,147 --> 00:32:59,229
[SPEAKER_01]: A domain of pieces of wood, as in domain of wood.

438
00:32:59,429 --> 00:33:01,691
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't clean my towel by washing my bucket.

439
00:33:01,811 --> 00:33:02,551
[SPEAKER_01]: No, exactly.

440
00:33:02,952 --> 00:33:14,920
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no amount of... doesn't matter how expensive the heap might become, it's still gonna be heap of five domains, five natures, five types of things.

441
00:33:15,721 --> 00:33:19,023
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the Buddha said, doesn't matter what... like the form,

442
00:33:19,370 --> 00:33:27,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter if it's a past form, future form, present form, personal, impersonal, internal, external, big or small, far or near, up or down.

443
00:33:28,036 --> 00:33:29,897
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows form is there.

444
00:33:30,858 --> 00:33:36,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter if it's a blue towel, a red towel, a yellow towel, a big towel, my towel, someone else's towel.

445
00:33:36,642 --> 00:33:37,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Towels are there.

446
00:33:39,083 --> 00:33:47,449
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't do this for the sake of some kind of exercise in your perception, because obviously you can do that in the simile I gave, as a heap of things that you can perceive.

447
00:33:48,162 --> 00:33:55,244
[SPEAKER_01]: But imagine how hard it becomes when it's your experience, it's heaped like that, where you can only perceive the perceptions.

448
00:33:57,044 --> 00:34:00,565
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't go and perceive in the same manner feeling and matter and other things.

449
00:34:00,865 --> 00:34:02,945
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha said that matter matters.

450
00:34:03,605 --> 00:34:05,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling feels.

451
00:34:05,246 --> 00:34:06,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception perceives.

452
00:34:07,706 --> 00:34:08,106
[SPEAKER_01]: And so on.

453
00:34:08,446 --> 00:34:09,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Consciousness cognizes.

454
00:34:09,747 --> 00:34:10,067
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why...

455
00:34:10,467 --> 00:34:13,428
[SPEAKER_01]: So consciousness doesn't go and cognize matter.

456
00:34:14,068 --> 00:34:15,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Consciousness cognizes.

457
00:34:15,696 --> 00:34:16,056
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

458
00:34:16,136 --> 00:34:18,377
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the extent of its... of its heap.

459
00:34:19,737 --> 00:34:20,557
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the five heaps.

460
00:34:20,997 --> 00:34:28,899
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, by having five heaps, completely independent, completely indifferent, completely unaware of each other.

461
00:34:29,859 --> 00:34:32,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Heap of towels is unaware of the heap of wood.

462
00:34:34,040 --> 00:34:37,041
[SPEAKER_01]: The heap of feelings is unaware of the heap of perceptions.

463
00:34:37,569 --> 00:34:38,469
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the five heaps.

464
00:34:38,689 --> 00:34:39,550
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the starting.

465
00:34:39,590 --> 00:34:40,390
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the necessary basis.

466
00:34:40,410 --> 00:34:41,250
[SPEAKER_00]: But we make the connection.

467
00:34:41,691 --> 00:34:46,312
[SPEAKER_01]: You assume the connection through the directionality of your ignorance with the grain.

468
00:34:48,373 --> 00:34:48,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh!

469
00:34:49,454 --> 00:34:51,414
[SPEAKER_01]: I have this feeling and I'm perceiving this.

470
00:34:52,035 --> 00:34:52,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah!

471
00:34:52,355 --> 00:34:54,235
[SPEAKER_01]: So I'm feeling this, what I'm perceiving.

472
00:34:54,656 --> 00:34:55,816
[SPEAKER_01]: And it becomes one thing.

473
00:34:55,836 --> 00:34:57,337
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes me and everything else.

474
00:34:58,397 --> 00:35:05,720
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you see, it doesn't matter if it's me, others, me and others, internal, external, internal and external, past, future, present.

475
00:35:06,999 --> 00:35:15,805
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is, whichever way it is, of those particular things I've just described, that's already within the nature of the five aggregates.

476
00:35:16,505 --> 00:35:32,136
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by seeing that no... like, whatever... whatever feels closest to you, whatever feels most personal to you, whatever feels most emotionally significant to you, you realize it's... it could have only arisen

477
00:35:32,737 --> 00:35:37,200
[SPEAKER_01]: On the basis of these five random heaps that you have no control over.

478
00:35:37,220 --> 00:35:53,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if there were no body, if there were no domain of feelings to be felt, if the domain of perception is not working, if the domain of consciousness is not cognizing, if the main intentions cannot intend, how could you be engaged with anything in any shape or form?

479
00:35:56,814 --> 00:35:58,175
[SPEAKER_01]: How could you be alive to begin with?

480
00:36:00,057 --> 00:36:01,758
[SPEAKER_01]: So then you realize that no matter

481
00:36:02,288 --> 00:36:09,234
[SPEAKER_01]: What emotional significance of what experience you might be dealing with, or trying to deal with, you realize it's secondary.

482
00:36:09,915 --> 00:36:11,956
[SPEAKER_01]: To the basis of this structure, there is no saying.

483
00:36:12,817 --> 00:36:14,158
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you lose concern.

484
00:36:15,319 --> 00:36:24,547
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't lose concern in your... in your ownership, in things that define you, in... in emotional attitudes by denying them.

485
00:36:25,027 --> 00:36:30,152
[SPEAKER_01]: You lose concern by uprooting them, by seeing that they're rooting in something that you cannot even...

486
00:36:32,029 --> 00:36:38,252
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot even slightest... you cannot even have a slightest conceiving of it as... as having a say in it.

487
00:36:38,372 --> 00:36:40,854
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot even control it to a slightest degree.

488
00:36:42,154 --> 00:36:57,322
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why this realization can very, very often be frightening for somebody who is still not letting go of that assumed ownership of the... of the experience as a whole, of me, my world and my environment.

489
00:36:58,183 --> 00:36:58,623
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why

490
00:36:59,758 --> 00:37:05,362
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, it's a mistake, but it's a gratuitous mistake that, as the Buddha said, it has no first point of manifestation.

491
00:37:05,623 --> 00:37:08,025
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, I wasn't ignorant, then I became ignorant.

492
00:37:08,065 --> 00:37:09,286
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that is the starting point.

493
00:37:10,166 --> 00:37:15,170
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why then people would just keep expanding on it, keep proliferating it further.

494
00:37:15,190 --> 00:37:23,757
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you already made the same mistake by assuming this experience as a whole to be... you assume that you have a say in it.

495
00:37:24,838 --> 00:37:28,801
[SPEAKER_01]: You assume that you have a say in the domain of feeling, the domain of perception, the domain of form.

496
00:37:29,407 --> 00:37:30,447
[SPEAKER_01]: The domain of cognizing.

497
00:37:31,928 --> 00:37:35,509
[SPEAKER_01]: And because you took that for granted, then you just extend the principle further.

498
00:37:36,490 --> 00:37:41,512
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, from this fundamental domain, I also assume I have a domain in...

499
00:37:41,612 --> 00:37:47,434
[SPEAKER_01]: I have a say in the domain of things that I perceive to be mine, that I have acquired.

500
00:37:48,494 --> 00:37:50,955
[SPEAKER_01]: If you acquired something, it clearly wasn't yours beforehand.

501
00:37:51,515 --> 00:37:54,656
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you could acquire it is because it wasn't yours beforehand.

502
00:37:55,337 --> 00:37:58,778
[SPEAKER_01]: If it were truly yours, if you were truly master, controller, the owner,

503
00:37:59,962 --> 00:38:01,682
[SPEAKER_01]: There will be no acquiring necessary.

504
00:38:02,583 --> 00:38:05,463
[SPEAKER_01]: So, acquiring is always a gratuitous appropriation.

505
00:38:06,584 --> 00:38:10,324
[SPEAKER_01]: When I say acquiring, I don't mean you take this and put it next to you because it's useful.

506
00:38:10,945 --> 00:38:12,925
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I mean sense of emotional ownership.

507
00:38:13,025 --> 00:38:17,586
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will know whether you have that emotional ownership, whereby, if you just... all you have to do is think about it.

508
00:38:17,606 --> 00:38:21,367
[SPEAKER_01]: If something comes and steals it, as the example you gave, will you be emotionally affected by it?

509
00:38:21,827 --> 00:38:22,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

510
00:38:22,187 --> 00:38:22,847
[SPEAKER_01]: To what extent?

511
00:38:23,088 --> 00:38:24,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Or if it changes.

512
00:38:24,128 --> 00:38:24,648
[SPEAKER_01]: If it changes.

513
00:38:24,708 --> 00:38:25,128
[SPEAKER_01]: If you lose it.

514
00:38:25,168 --> 00:38:26,128
[SPEAKER_01]: If it stops functioning.

515
00:38:26,408 --> 00:38:27,028
[SPEAKER_00]: I feel great.

516
00:38:27,189 --> 00:38:27,769
[SPEAKER_00]: I feel great.

517
00:38:27,869 --> 00:38:28,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

518
00:38:28,209 --> 00:38:28,529
[SPEAKER_00]: I feel great.

519
00:38:28,549 --> 00:38:28,869
[SPEAKER_00]: And then...

520
00:38:29,763 --> 00:38:33,905
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you never forget, that means you never really assume the ownership of things.

521
00:38:34,065 --> 00:38:35,085
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can use all you want.

522
00:38:36,286 --> 00:38:37,426
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be blinded by it.

523
00:38:37,586 --> 00:38:38,727
[SPEAKER_01]: But that takes undoing.

524
00:38:40,367 --> 00:38:42,228
[SPEAKER_01]: And, and, like, people can take that even further.

525
00:38:42,248 --> 00:38:44,789
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, there is no end to how far you can proliferate this.

526
00:38:45,209 --> 00:38:49,171
[SPEAKER_01]: To the point where somebody said something to somebody else, somewhere else.

527
00:38:49,971 --> 00:38:52,092
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm emotionally affected because of that.

528
00:38:52,973 --> 00:38:53,773
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's one thing.

529
00:38:54,336 --> 00:38:55,337
[SPEAKER_01]: That I took for granted.

530
00:38:55,477 --> 00:39:09,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I want to emotionally act out of it and make sure that that person, who is unaware of my existence, had nothing to do with me, suffers on account of what he said to somebody else, somewhere else, that upset me on a completely different continent.

531
00:39:11,389 --> 00:39:19,895
[SPEAKER_01]: And as I said, it's just a proliferation of the same, the most fundamental existential principle of turning a blind eye to the basic truth that your experience as a whole is not your own.

532
00:39:20,543 --> 00:39:24,966
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you need to keep maintaining your sense of ownership in regard to it, because it isn't yours.

533
00:39:26,848 --> 00:39:29,590
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's truly what imper... recognition of impermanence is.

534
00:39:30,030 --> 00:39:32,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Means recognition of impossibility to own.

535
00:39:34,094 --> 00:39:39,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were the owner, things would last as long as you would want them to last, not as long they would want to last.

536
00:39:40,178 --> 00:39:41,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Always trying to hold things together.

537
00:39:41,639 --> 00:39:41,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

538
00:39:41,920 --> 00:39:42,360
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

539
00:39:42,660 --> 00:39:43,721
[SPEAKER_01]: It requires maintenance.

540
00:39:45,082 --> 00:39:48,785
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the... the... eh...

541
00:39:49,763 --> 00:39:50,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Upādāna is holding.

542
00:39:51,184 --> 00:39:54,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Can also be good, you know, just holding together.

543
00:39:54,586 --> 00:39:54,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Holding.

544
00:39:55,467 --> 00:40:04,192
[SPEAKER_01]: But I prefer the word assumption for it, because that's... Holding implies you know what you hold, or you might like, oh, I better drop this.

545
00:40:05,033 --> 00:40:09,676
[SPEAKER_01]: But assumption is much less intentional, yet it's something you're fully responsible for.

546
00:40:10,316 --> 00:40:15,219
[SPEAKER_01]: And assumption literally means to take things for granted, to take things at face value.

547
00:40:15,980 --> 00:40:19,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, experience a reason, must be mine, because I'm the only one here.

548
00:40:20,725 --> 00:40:22,346
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I am the owner of this experience.

549
00:40:22,366 --> 00:40:23,707
[SPEAKER_01]: You just gratuitously assume that.

550
00:40:25,869 --> 00:40:29,212
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why assumption is irreversible if you undo it.

551
00:40:30,093 --> 00:40:34,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can only assume something if you don't know what that thing truly is.

552
00:40:35,337 --> 00:40:40,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Because once you do know what that thing truly is, assumption is cancelled.

553
00:40:42,288 --> 00:40:43,169
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes redundant.

554
00:40:43,649 --> 00:40:59,741
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you know that this thing cannot be mine, no amount of you saying to you, this is mine, and trying to rekindle the assumption of this is mine, can take place, because now you fully know that all these attitudes are in response to things not being able to be mine in the first place.

555
00:40:59,761 --> 00:41:03,764
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can only take something for granted until you know what that thing is.

556
00:41:03,784 --> 00:41:05,925
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why vijja cancels avijja.

557
00:41:08,247 --> 00:41:09,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Vijja is irreversible.

558
00:41:10,289 --> 00:41:11,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Once arahant becomes arahant, he cannot

559
00:41:13,078 --> 00:41:15,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Undo the state of arahantship and go back to being a patujana.

560
00:41:16,160 --> 00:41:16,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

561
00:41:20,563 --> 00:41:27,107
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you were taking things rightly, so to speak, the fact that you were taking them at face value, that's already what assumption is.

562
00:41:28,587 --> 00:41:35,111
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can have right assumptions, sure, but because it's still an assumption, to that extent, it's a wrong thing.

563
00:41:35,351 --> 00:41:36,132
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a wrong view.

564
00:41:42,462 --> 00:41:45,184
[SPEAKER_01]: To that extent, it's a perversion.

565
00:41:46,265 --> 00:41:48,766
[SPEAKER_01]: It's sort of distortion of the right order.

566
00:41:51,188 --> 00:41:52,269
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you had the right idea.

567
00:41:52,789 --> 00:41:53,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

568
00:41:53,810 --> 00:41:57,332
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you replace the wrong assumptions with right assumptions.

569
00:41:57,352 --> 00:42:06,859
[SPEAKER_01]: You replace the wrong views with the right view, in the terms of... you replace the worldly views with the right worldly view, which is still fundamentally the wrong view.

570
00:42:07,700 --> 00:42:11,022
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, on account of the right worldly view, you get the right right view.

571
00:42:12,175 --> 00:42:12,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it becomes clear.

572
00:42:12,915 --> 00:42:13,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

573
00:42:13,796 --> 00:42:15,257
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that our assumptions are bad.

574
00:42:15,277 --> 00:42:16,137
[SPEAKER_01]: I must not assume anything.

575
00:42:16,538 --> 00:42:19,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, that's already within your assumption, what assumptions are.

576
00:42:20,460 --> 00:42:31,006
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to find the right... Firstly, for Putrajana, it's right to assume that he must restrain himself, physically, verbally, in order to get to deal with problems on the level of mind.

577
00:42:31,506 --> 00:42:35,068
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the right thing to assume that liberation is possible.

578
00:42:36,829 --> 00:42:41,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it were impossible, you wouldn't even have the slightest notion towards any sense of freedom.

579
00:42:42,179 --> 00:42:50,163
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that you do have a sense of... like notions of a sense of freedom from desire, sense of freedom from suffering... Yeah, you don't know if it's possible, but you assume that it's possible.

580
00:42:51,104 --> 00:42:55,506
[SPEAKER_01]: And to that extent, it's a right assumption that can eventually result in undoing all the assumptions.

581
00:42:55,526 --> 00:42:58,868
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha meant craving is to be undone by the means of craving.

582
00:42:58,908 --> 00:43:02,050
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same principle in regard to the topic of craving.

583
00:43:05,332 --> 00:43:10,995
[SPEAKER_01]: But what will never result in undoing craving is if you never start understanding the nature of craving, if you never start

584
00:43:11,460 --> 00:43:16,301
[SPEAKER_01]: What will never result in undoing the assumption is if you never start to discern the nature of present assumption.

585
00:43:16,921 --> 00:43:20,562
[SPEAKER_01]: If you just divide it into wrong assumptions and right assumptions.

586
00:43:21,443 --> 00:43:22,923
[SPEAKER_01]: This is wrong craving, this is right craving.

587
00:43:22,943 --> 00:43:23,323
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

588
00:43:23,343 --> 00:43:24,603
[SPEAKER_01]: That's as far as your work went.

589
00:43:26,384 --> 00:43:30,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, you just replaced bad with good, but you're equally bound by it.

590
00:43:31,665 --> 00:43:35,186
[SPEAKER_01]: You're equally bound by kamma, by the distortion of the notion of action.

591
00:43:36,847 --> 00:43:38,727
[SPEAKER_01]: By the distortion of the notion that you are acting.

592
00:43:40,103 --> 00:43:42,465
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that, you need to go beyond good as well.

593
00:43:43,206 --> 00:43:45,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Beyond good cravings, beyond good upādānas.

594
00:43:46,028 --> 00:43:47,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Beyond restraint, in a way.

595
00:43:47,229 --> 00:43:48,850
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why arahant is beyond training.

596
00:43:49,731 --> 00:43:53,474
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nothing for him to train anymore, to say no to, to restrain.

597
00:43:53,674 --> 00:43:53,794
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

598
00:43:54,014 --> 00:43:54,555
[SPEAKER_01]: He's beyond it.

599
00:43:54,715 --> 00:43:56,336
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable to do even that.

600
00:43:59,218 --> 00:44:01,820
[SPEAKER_01]: But because it's irreversible, he can't do anything unwholesome either.

601
00:44:02,061 --> 00:44:05,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that was abandoned way before he even abandoned good.

602
00:44:06,102 --> 00:44:10,363
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you would say, like, his behavior has been purified beforehand.

603
00:44:11,363 --> 00:44:20,046
[SPEAKER_01]: When purified behavior, when right assumptions, when right types of craving become your mode of being, means you have replaced the bad mode of being.

604
00:44:20,646 --> 00:44:26,368
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, on account of the right mode of being, you get to discern the further, that nature of that distortion of those

605
00:44:28,450 --> 00:44:33,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamental existential assumptions, and by undoing it, then you go beyond good action as well.

606
00:44:33,312 --> 00:44:36,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, arahant is beyond karma of any kind, good, bad or neutral.

607
00:44:38,073 --> 00:44:42,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he's beyond... he has undone all the... any distortion that there was.

